SM(478,{"n":"Acts 20","a":"Fr Stephen De Young Archives/Acts 20.mp3","d":2543.5,"s":[[0.4,"Come and study Holy Scripture with us as Father Stephen DeYoung teaches verse by verse on the podcast The Whole Counsel of God."],[8.4,"Father Stephen is a former Protestant pastor and Bible teacher in the Reformed tradition, but now an Orthodox priest serving at Archangel Gabriel Orthodox Church in Lafayette, Louisiana."],[21.76,"When we get started in just a minute, we'll be picking up at the beginning of uh chapter 20 in Book of the Acts of the Apostles."],[33.12,"Um, as I now always say, because it's been slightly altered now, you can go and listen to the first Bible study on the Book of Acts online at Ancient Faith Radio, uh, because it is now posted there."],[45.76,"We've caught up that close at least."],[47.92,"Um, so if you want to go back and hear the introduction to the book, uh you can do that there."],[54.24,"Um in chapter one, I think, too."],[56.96,"But um So to get us caught up to uh where we were last time, uh Saint Paul is on essentially his third sort of missionary journey."],[71.4,"Uh he came to uh Ephesus uh last time and uh Arthur has helpfully brought us a visual aid in the form of a uh Chochke from from Ephesus."],[86.36,"Uh which is the uh this is a uh designed for tourists, a uh replica of uh what the uh goddess Artemis, as she was worshipped at Ephesus, looked like."],[101.16,"We commented last time on the fact that she looks a little different than any other other statue of Artemis you'd find anywhere else in the Greek or Roman world because she had been sort of schmushed together with Eastern mother goddess figures."],[114.88,"Um we talked about how Roman culture sort of assimilated a lot of the religious beliefs of of uh conquered territory."],[123.76,"Uh sort of brought it all together and just said, Oh, well, this is really these are really all the same."],[128.8,"You know, gods just by different names."],[131.36,"Um that was a that was a strong approach when you're looking to sort of religiously unify a diver empirese of peoples."],[141.32,"If you could just sort of say, ah, it's all the same, you know."],[144.4,"And you can let sort of whatever local festivals they have go on, you know, and just file it all. all in as oh, this is part of one part of one big thing"],[154.68,"Um we tend to talk about it as one big thing."],[157.64,"We talk about paganism as if it was like a religion, yeah, that had like leaders or something, and that's not the case."],[166.44,"What we call paganism is just a hodgepodge of different uh polytheistic belief systems from all different parts of the world."],[176.36,"Um, and so the one of the reasons though why we think of it as this united thing is precisely because of the Romans."],[184.48,"Because they sort of successfully propagandized the empire that it was all really the same, um so we."],[194.48,"So we read about how Saint Paul ran into some trouble there along with his fellow travelers because tourism to come to the Temple of Artemis at Ephesus, Temple of Artemis at Ephesus was one of the seven wonders of the ancient world."],[212.0,"And so people literally came from all over the Roman world to come and see it, to come there for festivals, to come there for celebrations, to come there to buy tchotkis and replicas of the statue to take home with them for their own religious worship."],[228.72,"And this was a huge part of obviously of the industry of the city."],[233.64,"Well, when Saint Paul uh and his fellow travelers come there, and they're there for a year and a half preaching.ing, not only in the synagogue, but publicly, and people are com to be Christians and to follow Christ"],[245.56,"Well, guess what?"],[246.36,"They're not going to the Temple of Artemis anymore."],[248.76,"They're getting rid of their idols of Artemis."],[251.88,"They aren't buying new ones from the Smiths and the Craftsmen in the town."],[255.88,"And so this begins to have an adverse effect on the local economy, which then leads the craftsman's guild, as we saw, to sort of rise up against St."],[266.68,"Paul and his fellow travelers to get them run out of town before this could before this could go any further."],[275.04,"Um so uh we talked last time about how this is sort of exhibit a of the way that you know we tend to because uh we live in a country that has a separation of church and state we tend to take that for granted we look at like politics like it's over here and economics is over here and religion is over here and you know these are these separate things and how in the ancient world that was not the case right in the ancient world these things are all deeply interwoven and interconnected together right so loyalty to Caesar and to the Roman government meant partaking in these religious ceremonies which were also a major part of the local economy, which of course is a major part of the functioning of the empire, the economy."],[320.86,"So these things all all are interconnected very deeply at this point in uh in history and for many centuries thereafter."],[331.26,"Um these remain intertwined."],[334.46,"We mentioned last time, and we'll get into this more in uh 1 Corinthians because this becomes a major issue, especially there, although it's an issue everywhere in the Roman Empire for the Gentile converts who come out of paganism."],[347.58,"Uh the meat, almost all of the meat that was sold in the meat markets in cities like Corinth and Athens uh was meat that had come from the animals sacrificed at the temples and the altars of the city that day."],[364.3,"Okay. Because they were continually sacrificing multiple animals every day."],[369.28,"To the pagan gods."],[370.76,"Um, and as we mentioned before, the Greeks used to call the sacrificial process the burning of the bones, because that was about the only part that they actually offered to the god were the parts they didn't have another use for."],[382.73,"The leather they took and they sold, and the meat they took and they sold."],[385.77,"Well, we got nothing to do with the bones."],[387.29,"We'll burn those and give them to the god."],[389.02,"So but this is another way that it's in the economy."],[393.52,"Your food supply of meat is connected to this."],[397.52,"And so that's going to become a major issue that uh St."],[401.28,"Paul's gonna have to deal with."],[402.4,"We already saw in Acts 15 that that was one of the things that the Gentile converts were told they couldn't do."],[407.62,"Was eat meat that had been offered to idols."],[410.02,"And so uh St."],[411.54,"Paul's gonna have to deal with that."],[413.62,"Um it's it's not just an issue then of although he brings this up too."],[420.46,"We we also talked before when we were talking about Corinth about how we've excavated like the temple of of Demeter at Corinth, and there are huge banquet halls, so they would have these huge feasts with the meat from the sacrifices, so it's not just an issue of okay, well, you can't you know go to the temple and participate in the feast anymore, which seems a little more obvious."],[442.88,"That now you're a Christian, you can't do that, but the meat they're selling in the meat market, that's where it came from."],[450.01,"It's been offered to a pagan god."],[452.89,"So was there any other source of meat uh in those cities, not really."],[458.95,"And so St."],[459.83,"Paul's gonna talk about those who eat only vegetables."],[463.39,"Uh-huh. And that's what lies behind it's not that there's no history of this."],[468.11,"This you read the book of Daniel, remember that Daniel and his three friends essentially had to become vegetarians when they were in Babylon for exactly this reason."],[478.93,"Because the meat that was being served at the palace had all been offered to pagan gods."],[486.61,"So they just became vegetarians."],[490.45,"Can you say, tell me, what was a sacrifice like?"],[493.97,"Did they bring like a little?"],[496.69,"And then they would just like sleep."],[498.29,"Well, so it was mostly it was mostly pigs."],[500.93,"Pigs were the most common animal."],[503.25,"Uh for both in Greek. and Roman sacrifices because they breed fast and grow fast"],[509.74,"Cattle takes a lot longer, you know, if you're doing an adult bull or something."],[514.94,"So they would sacrifice bulls, but that would be for sort of more major occasions or for wealthy people, you know, or if you had some important senator or the emperor visiting, you know, or an important general, you know, you'd bring out the more expensive stuff."],[529.66,"But then most of the day-to-day days day-to-day stuff was was pigs."],[534.22,"Um and uh basically the animals uh were there there was there was first a procession that was held where the animal would be sort of led to where it was going to be killed."],[551.3,"Um and that procession would usually have be led by the priest, but there would also be women and and other people who would be playing instruments and singing hymns and that kind of thing."],[565.86,"And they bring the animal to the place where they were gonna kill it, and then there was a ceremony around killing it where they'd have a fire, and uh this part of this actually got incorporated into our baptismal service."],[579.78,"They would grab a clump of the the animal's hair and cut its hair off, cut the hair off and throw it into a fire pot."],[588.26,"And that's in our baptismal service with when we when we cut the hair and then throw it into the the sens sensoror. and put it in"],[597.5,"And so that's part of the symbolism of what's going on in the baptismal service that a lot of people don't get now because we haven't seen a lot of pagan animal sacrifices, is that it represents either if it's a baby, you know, the parents offering the child or the person offering themselves as a sacrifice to God."],[612.16,"Now obviously we don't kill them afterwards, but it it represents that."],[617.74,"Um and so then the the uh the animal would be killed uh usually with a knife by slitting its throat."],[626.7,"Um and the blood would be drained out, and they'd take the blood in bowls one place that I'll say where in a minute."],[634.38,"But then the animal would be taken uh to a place uh that was referred to. in Greek as the prothesis, the place of preparation"],[642.9,"And it's again, it's not a coincidence that the table behind our icotostasis where we prepare the bread and the wine before the Eucharist is called the prothesis."],[655.38,"And that's where the animal would be taken and would be essentially butchered."],[660.12,"But the the parts would be separated out."],[663.48,"So that the the uh the parts that were gonna go to the priests, the parts usually the Greek and Roman case, the useless parts that were that were going to go to the god that were gonna be burnt, um, the parts that were gonna go back to the people who brought the sacrifice, the parts that were going to the banquet hall, if there was one, the parts that were going to the meat market."],[681.1,"All of that would be separated out."],[684.18,"And in that process, uh for Roman sacrifices, this wasn't done as much by the Greeks."],[691.22,"It's a huge with the Romans, though."],[693.06,"They'd had a guy called a harospex, and uh his job was to come and inspect the entrails of the animal because they believed that they would see omens and predict the future and this kind of thing by seeing the omens of the animal."],[707.78,"Now, it wasn't just predict the future in a vague sense, it was usually whatever they were offering the sacrifice for."],[713.11,"So if they were about to have a go into battle, and they offered sacrifice for that, he would come and based on what he saw, he would either say, Okay, things look good or things look bad."],[722.78,"In a more general case where they were just offering daily sacrifices, he'd look at it based on that, he could decide whether the God was going to accept it or not."],[731.06,"And if he saw something in there that said the God wasn't going to accept it, then they'd have to figure out what the problem was."],[737.96,"Why is the God upset?"],[739.4,"And a lot of times that was it must be these Christians."],[742.85,"And before that, it was it must be these Jews who aren't participating."],[746.94,"That's got the God so bad."],[748.46,"But so he'd come and read that."],[750.06,"And we have like stone livers with markings all over them that were used for like training by these guys that told them what to look for."],[762.3,"Like livers and kidneys and hearts and stuff that are marked up, like here's here's what to look for when you examine the stuff."],[768.78,"So they would do that in the process, too while it was being butchered, and then the part that parts that were being offered to the god would be taken to the altar and burned, and uh the other parts would be distributed."],[782.58,"And uh Greek and Roman temples had sort of two sections."],[787.62,"They had uh a section that was dedicated to uh to the god in question, the pagan god in question, but then they would also have a shrine to uh this is where we get the word hero to to uh a local hero."],[807.03,"Um, and again, this is where we get the word hero."],[809.83,"Um, and that would be someone usually it was the founder of the city, but sometimes it was some kind of mythical figure."],[817.49,"Who was uh like Pericles or you know one of these uh Pericles wasn't mythical."],[823.41,"Um who was I thinking of?"],[824.85,"Perseus. Uh Pericles was the real person in Athens."],[829.57,"Uh but Perseus, you know, some of these sort of mythical semi or semi-mythical founders, um, or uh great departed generals, that kind of thing, would have a shrine there, and that shrine is usually where they were buried."],[844.93,"Um and the the Greeks believed and the Romans that when you died you went into Hades, into the grave, and you continue to exist as uh it's usually translated as shade, but it really means shadow."],[858.69,"shadow. You're sort of literally a of your former self"],[863.13,"And that sort of shadowy version of you, this isn't a great afterlife, but that sort of shadowy version of you would continue to exist for as long as people remembered you."],[875.05,"Wow. Okay. That explains the glory colour."],[878.81,"And that if you were sort of forgotten, then you'd sort of cease to exist."],[883.7,"Um they believed in the same way that the the that ancient Israel believed, says to the Old Testament, that blood sort of contained life within it."],[895.96,"Because when it comes out of like an animal, it's hot."],[898.73,"And then it cools off once it's dead, you know."],[900.61,"You know, so they thought, well, this has the life in it."],[901.85,"the life in it. They thought the heat was sort of that was still"],[906.45,"And so they believed they would take that blood and they would from the sacrifice and they pour it onto the grave of this hero because they believed that would sort of reinvigorate this sort of shadowy existence."],[920.13,"And this, you know, that's that was their practice."],[922.77,"You see this reflected like in uh Homer's Odyssey."],[926.93,"There's a there's a scene where uh Odysseus needs to talk to uh Achilles, who's of course died in the Iliad."],[935.81,"So so he goes to this cave that's supposed to be a gateway to Hades, and he sacrifices a bunch of black goats just to make it creepier."],[944.93,"Black goats Yeah, a bunch of black goats."],[947.09,"ch And drains their blood sort of into this dit."],[947.17,"of And sort all the shades come and start drinking, start lapping up the blood."],[955.77,"And so that sort of draws out Achilles so he can talk to him."],[960.41,"But you asked Lily."],[968.49,"No, they didn't drink it."],[969.53,"They gave it to the dead."],[971.29,"Which is, I think, even creepier."],[973.05,"But so the dead could drink it."],[975.61,"Yeah. Um, but so yeah, that's how uh that's how a pagan sacrifice worked."],[982.33,"Okay. Um that's gonna be important to remember, like I said, when we get into 1 Corinthians and 2nd Corinthians, and St."],[989.53,"Paul's talking about food offered to idols, and he's talking about things involving the dead and this kind of thing."],[997.97,"We need to remember, you know."],[1002.05,"There was some crabs or fruits, did they do that?"],[1005.97,"Yeah, they um they did less of that than the Jews did."],[1010.21,"The Romans in particular uh did a lot of stuff with uh cakes."],[1013.89,"They make like meal cakes that were sacrificed."],[1017.33,"Um it wasn't always meat, it wasn't they didn't always kill an animal, uh, but it was always food."],[1022.37,"Food, yeah. Yeah, it was always food."],[1026.21,"Yeah, it it's hard."],[1027.49,"Some of it we don't know for sure, because you know, we find the remains of things like in these banquet halls, but we don't know if all of the food there was sacrificed, or if like they'd bring in the meat and the cakes and stuff, and then they also just had other food that they served with it."],[1046.39,"We're not totally sure what's what."],[1049.99,"Um but yeah, there were things other than animals that were off."],[1054.55,"In the case of Israel, the sacrificial animals are usually lambs, uh, they were sheep."],[1059.19,"Sheep and goats."],[1060.15,"Sheep and goats."],[1060.95,"Yeah. And um the Israelites aren't allowed to eat pork."],[1064.87,"Pork, so that's why there's no sacrificing pigs."],[1067.75,"Yeah. But if they're if they're living in these Greek cities where most of the meat on sale is pork, then they have a problem."],[1075.03,"Well, yeah, the Jews rather."],[1076.51,"So that's why it's a problem for the Gentile converts."],[1080.85,"Because they haven't they haven't been told you can't eat pork."],[1081.25,"the So Gentiles didn't have to refrain from eating pork necessarily, or the other animals that were forbidden."],[1086.09,"Remember, we went through Acts 15."],[1094.31,"But they were forbidden from eating meat offered to idols."],[1097.59,"So the question is, well, okay, yeah, obviously that means I can't go to the temple for the festival and eat there, and participate."],[1105.69,"But does that include this meat that's now out here in the meat market?"],[1112.49,"Where I'm not going to the temple."],[1114.97,"I'm not offering it to an idol."],[1116.65,"I'm not having anything to do with that."],[1118.09,"I'm just eating this meat that happens to have gone through there in the process."],[1122.65,"Would this be a large part of their meat diet would be would be what they would get from the temple?"],[1129.73,"That was in a lot of these cities like Corinth and Athens, that was pretty much the only meat available."],[1134.45,"So if they were unless you were a farmer and had your own animals and were killing them yourself."],[1137.89,"So if they weren't of a lot of meat, they they weren't wealthy."],[1142.05,"This meant becoming a vegetarian."],[1143.89,"Or eating fish exclusively."],[1145.81,"If you could even afford it."],[1147.01,"Yeah. Okay. So this could cut even into your dietary animals."],[1150.53,"Yeah. And one of the one of the really interesting things, and I don't want to digress on this too much, but uh as far as we could tell, nobody ever sacrificed fish."],[1160.13,"Even to even to water gods."],[1162.45,"Like you think like Poseidon and stuff, they might go and sacrifice their fish."],[1167.09,"Nobody ever sacrificed fish."],[1168.69,"The the the going theory by researchers is that it's because they aren't warm blooded."],[1170.69,"Ah, so they don't have life"],[1176.41,"Yeah, they associated this life with the warm blood."],[1180.73,"And so they didn't say the fish and stuff like that because it didn't have that."],[1187.37,"Wow. Okay. So which is also um I think that ancient categorization is also part of what's going on with our fasting rules, why fish isn't considered meat."],[1198.25,"There's like meat and then there's fish."],[1202.33,"And you're like, well, isn't that really meat?"],[1204.65,"Well, it wasn't really considered to be in ancient times."],[1210.01,"Um like seafood wasn't considered meat in ancient times."],[1215.45,"It was this very great."],[1218.45,"This uh this other kind of thing."],[1219.81,"Here here in Louisiana especially."],[1221.81,"Yeah. Prothesis. Yeah, prothesis."],[1234.85,"Where we prepare things, yeah."],[1235.97,"Like that was something that was done in ancient times that's what's carried over to today."],[1239.73,"Yeah. So what's one other thing that we do today that was like something?"],[1244.05,"Well, ever everything everything around that is related to that."],[1249.17,"Around the the when we when we celebrate the Eucharist, we're celebrating it as a sacrifice."],[1256.09,"That's the it's our our liturgical translation. talks about unbloody service, and you're like, that kind of hides what it means"],[1264.85,"What it really means in the original language is bloodless sacrifice, meaning we're not killing anything."],[1270.89,"We don't have to because Christ died once for all."],[1275.63,"And we're not killing him again."],[1277.76,"Um, and so, but we're we're uh we're offering a uh a thank offering."],[1284.73,"Because a lot a lot of times we've boiled down into our heads that sort of all the sacrifices in the Old Testament were sin offerings, and they were all killing animals."],[1295.07,"And that's not true."],[1296.13,"As we were just talking about."],[1297.73,"You go, there were grain offerings, drink offerings, first fruits. all kinds of offerings made in the Old Testament"],[1304.33,"So, yes, Christ offered himself as a sin offering."],[1308.43,"On behalf of the whole world for everyone for all time."],[1313.07,"But there are all of these other sacrifices and offerings."],[1319.87,"And that's what the word Eucharist that we call that means thanksgiving."],[1325.87,"So we call it a thank offering right off the bat."],[1329.49,"Where we come and we offer uh we offer uh the fruits of our hands."],[1336.3,"Baked bread and wine."],[1339.32,"We offer those to God, and in return we receive the body and blood of Christ."],[1343.96,"Um, but so like when we're when we're at the prothesis preparing the bread, we do it the same way"],[1350.57,"I mean, there's a piece that's cut out to use for the Eucharist."],[1355.07,"There's a and and out of that, there's a piece of that that goes that's eaten by the priest."],[1359.89,"Just like in the Old Testament sacrifices, there's a portion eaten by the priest, a portion eaten by the people who brought the sacrifice."],[1365.18,"A portion offered to God."],[1366.88,"All of that is still there."],[1370.8,"Um and so everything we do there, and in regard to that, if and and you know, we do the great entrance, we process in with it up to the altar."],[1380.32,"Any ancient person."],[1382.34,"We could take an ancient Greek or an ancient Roman from before Christianity, who didn't know any English."],[1389.78,"And and bring them here, and if they watched the liturgy, they would know exactly what we were doing."],[1394.42,"They would say, Oh, they're sacrificing bread and wine to their God."],[1398.75,"That would be what they would that would be what they would say."],[1401.31,"They'd be like, they'd say, I don't know why, but I don't know, because I don't understand the language they're speaking, but it's pretty obvious what they're doing."],[1411.4,"Um so yeah, I mean that's the the liturgy itself."],[1418.76,"Yeah. And it we didn't get it from pagan sacrificial rites, we got it from the the sacrificial rites in the temple, is where we got it."],[1431.08,"Um but the the what was done at the Jewish temple was not so radically different in terms of how it was done."],[1438.46,"In terms of procedure, was not so radically different from other sacrifices that they wouldn't recognize it."],[1445.71,"They didn't, they didn't uh when the ancient Greeks and Romans, for example, or Babylonians or anyone else came to Jerusalem and saw the temple, they didn't say, wow, they really do their sacrifices in a weird way."],[1457.65,"That's not what they thought was weird."],[1460.29,"What they thought was weird was well, they only have this one temple and they only have one God."],[1464.77,"That was weird."],[1466.18,"And when uh the Roman general Pompeii went into the Holy of Holies and saw that there was no image of the god there."],[1473.98,"That was weird."],[1475.88,"But they didn't think, you know, wow, they do weird rituals."],[1482.04,"They were different, but they were still recognizable, you know, uh, and understandable."],[1489.4,"Um, so yeah, we we get it from the temple."],[1493.32,"Um and and there are some some scholars who have written on this um recently."],[1499.16,"Uh even scholars who aren't Orthodox who say that the traditional Christian liturgy you see in the East and way back the early Latin liturgy in the West basically reflects what was done in the Jewish temple, even in some of its details."],[1517.2,"The difference is we're doing it everywhere."],[1519.78,"Instead of just in one place."],[1521.55,"And we're not killing any animals."],[1524.43,"I've read some Roman authors who consider the Jews and the Christians to be atheists."],[1531.07,"Because with all the gods there are, they only worship one."],[1534.75,"Well. Well, well, it wasn't just that, it's that they only they refuse to worship to acknowledge the other ones."],[1540.57,"Yes. Uh huh."],[1541.47,"Because there may have been Greeks and Romans who just had one God who they primarily worshipped and served themselves personally."],[1547.55,"But they acknowledged all that knowledge all of them."],[1549.63,"Yeah, and if they were visiting a town, if they're visiting Ephesus, they'd go and pay their respects and you know, put pay some money and have them offer a pig for him at Artemis' temple and stuff."],[1559.07,"It wasn't exclusive."],[1560.15,"Yeah. It wasn't like, well, if I go and do that for Artemis, I'm betraying Mars."],[1565.73,"Or whatever. Right."],[1569.27,"Okay. Patrons saying."],[1571.19,"And it was just accepted that the the gods, the different gods, we think of these pantheons because that's how we learned about it as kids, you know."],[1581.27,"And they have all these interrelations."],[1583.11,"That's not the reality of what paganism was for most Greeks and Romans."],[1586.95,"They had a god or two who they worshipped, and that was primarily based on where they were from."],[1594.47,"Okay. Because cities had a god or two."],[1599.38,"Where they live now."],[1601.64,"And their family."],[1601.96,"their family. What gods have been important to, you know"],[1607.7,"Uh, cause because grandpa Gaius, you know, went to war and he was worried he was going to die in battle, so he offered a sacrifice to this god and he made it back."],[1618.34,"Okay. So ever since then, our family is honored."],[1621.3,"Okay. You know, whoever."],[1623.06,"Even local heroes would play a role in that too."],[1625.38,"Well, they didn't receive Cyrus directly, but they would be honored, you know, they might have images and that kind of thing."],[1630.98,"But so, yeah, that's that's how that came to be."],[1633.62,"It wasn't so much picking a favorite as it was based on, and so that's another way that these things were intertwined."],[1641.72,"So if you become a Christian now and you say, I'm not going to sacrifice to that God."],[1646.12,"You're spitting on that"],[1646.92,"What about Grandpa Gaius?"],[1649.48,"This God saved his life."],[1652.04,"You're spitting on our family."],[1654.04,"You know. So a lot of the early Christians found themselves thrown out of their families, thrown out of their communities, because you know, everyone in this city goes to the festival of you know."],[1666.12,"So so you know, even couldn't sell anything at the market if you tried."],[1670.46,"That's so they they found themselves, you know, complete social pariahs."],[1675.5,"Yeah. Um, because of that."],[1678.54,"So, yeah, all these things were deeply intertwined with the religion."],[1684.7,"Um, even down to family, family life."],[1688.78,"We just take it for granted that we can just oh, well, pick a church and go."],[1690.7,"I mean, there is there is still a little of that"],[1694.26,"Take it for granted."],[1695.38,"Yeah. I mean, there are some families that are staunchly something."],[1701.06,"Oh, yeah. And if you leave whatever that is, you're gone."],[1707.06,"A lot of people of that."],[1708.58,"Yeah. But but yeah, that was certainly the case for paganism in the ancient world."],[1715.94,"You know. And this the stakes, the stakes were higher because it not only involved, you know, your family and their sort of history and stuff, but it involved, you know, the future health and well being of that family as far as that family was concerned."],[1730.74,"Because the gods would no longer be eligible to that."],[1734.1,"So it's not just, oh, you've you've gone. and joined a cult, so I don't want to hang around with you anymore"],[1739.02,"It's you've gone and joined a cult, and so that's endangering our family, endangering our city."],[1745.58,"Potentially endangering the empire."],[1747.42,"Get away from us."],[1748.3,"Yeah. Yeah. Well, or we need to get rid of you."],[1751.9,"Yeah. To make the gods happy again."],[1755.89,"So that yeah, that's that's all woven together here."],[1760.53,"And we should we should keep that in mind as we're going through Acts here, that when we read about these pagans converting, you know, w how high the stakes are."],[1773.49,"And as we can see from what's going on in the synagogues, they're becoming very high with the Jewish community to,o."],[1779.57,"And that's going to reach a crisis point at the beginning of the second century when the synagogues expel all the Christians"],[1786.65,"But it's already starting here."],[1789.51,"Oh, you join those troublemakers."],[1791.68,"So the people who are choosing when Christ says, you know, he who loves father or mother more than me is not worthy of me."],[1800.08,"And you those kind of things, that was a reality for a lot of these earlier, these early Christians reading the book of Acts, both Jewish and pagan."],[1813.44,"When they become Christians, they're having to choose."],[1816.16,"Am I going to become a Christian and follow Christ?"],[1818.72,"Because that's my parents are going to disown, you know, my family's going to disown me me, cast out."],[1822.24,"cast me out. You know the city and the community is going to, you know"],[1833.0,"To have this effect."],[1834.36,"I mean, really."],[1835.88,"But but of course, what he's preaching is so startling."],[1841.0,"Yeah. Uh, I mean, here's here's here's uh one God who controls the whole universe, who cares about us and makes a sacrifice of himself for us."],[1856.04,"This must have been truly, truly shocking."],[1860.44,"Well, just just the idea."],[1862.88,"Just the idea that there's one God who created the universe and he knows your name."],[1867.28,"Uh-huh. And loveses you and car about you."],[1868.12,"Not even regardless as just at all"],[1869.92,"Uh-huh. Regardless of universal status."],[1875.6,"Right. You know?"],[1877.76,"Like, I mean, the the Greek and Roman gods didn't know who you were."],[1881.92,"They might know who the emperor was."],[1883.36,"But even that, if they did know who he notice you, that could go either way."],[1886.88,"Yeah. They could decide to destroy you."],[1890.72,"You know, and most often that's how their human interactions went."],[1894.88,"You know, it did not go well for the human."],[1896.64,"That turned into something."],[1897.92,"Yeah. Yeah. Or or, you know, chained to a rock and you know pushing a rock up a hill."],[1902.72,"Or um So yeah, I mean that's revolutionary."],[1907.52,"But what's also going to happen is that the the surrounding Roman world is going to see Christians from all different backgrounds being willing to suffer, being willing to die rather than turn their back on Christianity."],[1921.8,"And that's also going to greatly impress them because none of them would be willing to die for you know yeah."],[1929.4,"I mean, if they went to a new town and they said you're not allowed to worship Artemis anymore, now you have to worship Athena, they'd be like, Okay."],[1938.28,"None of them are gonna suffer for that, none of them are gonna die for that."],[1941.56,"Yeah. So the idea that there's some truth."],[1945.76,"There's some truth that you're willing to die and be tortured to death rather than deny."],[1952.78,"That's gonna pique a lot of their curiosity."],[1954.9,"Like, well, what is this all about if these people are willing to die?"],[1959.14,"The executioner converting, or something like that."],[1962.42,"Yeah, or bystanders."],[1963.62,"Uh-huh. Yeah. Yeah."],[1966.18,"Yeah. And that goes on even to this day."],[1968.34,"The uh the Coptic Christians who were uh martyred by ISIS on the beach."],[1977.14,"Uh I've got the icon of that in my office, and you'll notice one of them is more darkly complexed than the others because he was actually one of the ISIS members."],[1986.26,"Uh huh. Wow."],[1987.38,"Who saw that they were they were getting yeah, saw that they were getting killed."],[1992.42,"And would deny Jesus and said, and he said, yeah."],[1997.22,"And he said uh he he said uh uh you know what he said they' their God is my God and they turned the camera on him and said whatd you just say and he said no and he went over and knelt down next to them and they killed him too."],[2013.58,"But uh but yeah that still happens that was just a couple years ago where he's a icon too, and you know I always wondered why one was dark, you know, he still he stands out in it, yeah, and I didn't know."],[2027.26,"He was one of the one of one of the ISIS members because they look at it said, you know, I'm willing to kill for what I believe, but I don't know that I'd be willing to get killed."],[2041.1,"You know, for what I believe."],[2042.54,"So, but that that's gonna have a very powerful, a very powerful effect um and so that's why there's gonna be that saying in the early church that the blood of the martyrs became the seat of the church that the more they try to stamp out Christianity because of the stand that the early Christians took it ended up growing we've already seen that in the book of Acts I mean that's what happened with Saint Stephen right we need to shut this guy up so we stone him to death it scatters everybody and everywhere they go they preach the gospel and so churches spring up everywhere um yeah and that's gonna that's gonna continue uh throughout the early church."],[2080.46,"Okay, so all that started out is getting us caught up to where we were."],[2085.02,"But I think we're now adequately caught up, so we can get started in Acts of the Apostles chapter 20, verse 1."],[2093.5,"After the uproar had ceased, this is the uproar in Ephesus."],[2098.24,"That was caused by the craftsman's killed."],[2100.4,"Paul called the disciples to himself, embraced them, and departed to go to Macedonia."],[2106.88,"So I don't have a map behind me, but if you have it in your head, remember Ephesus is on the western coast of what's now Turkey, Asia Minor."],[2118.25,"And so he's talking about sailing across to Macedonia, which is here at the northern part of Greece."],[2127.53,"And so he says his goodbyes and goes over to Macedonia"],[2131.89,"He's sort of retracing his route from his last journey."],[2135.97,"And when he had gone over that region and encouraged them with many words, he came to Greece and stayed three months."],[2145.17,"So he's going back to the churches that he sort of planted on his way through the last time and is encouraging them and preaching there."],[2154.93,"And when the Jews plotted against him as he was about to sail to Syria, he decided to return through Macedonia."],[2161.97,"So his original plan after he'd gone through Greece for three months preaching at those churches was he was going to go back to Antioch, which has been sort of his home base."],[2171.17,"But he finds out that there are some folks from the synagogue who are still unhappy with him there and who are planning something."],[2180.13,"And so he says, W.ell, instead of taking the route I originally planned, I'll go back the way I came, and maybe we can avoid them"],[2188.65,"Because once again, St."],[2189.93,"Paul is not trying to cause these issues and cause these problems."],[2198.01,"And so Pater of Berea accompanied him to Asia."],[2201.37,"Also Aristarchus and Secundus of the Thessalonians, and Gaius of Derby, and Timothy and Tichus and Trophimus of Asia."],[2212.09,"So this is basically listing his travel companions."],[2216.25,"And we can see a lot of them are from cities that he's visited."],[2219.55,"Thessalonica and Berea and Derby."],[2223.71,"There's a Romanesque church in France of Saint Trophine, and I always wondered who that was"],[2230.47,"That's the same."],[2231.67,"Yeah. And remember when Saint Luke isn't just listing these names to be thorough."],[2238.01,"A lot of times when St."],[2239.05,"Luke lists these names, it's because remember, he's writing as a historian."],[2244.01,"These are people who at the time he wrote were still alive."],[2248.33,"So these are he's telling you who the witnesses are to the story he's about to tell."],[2255.45,"So you could go ask these people."],[2258.25,"He has testimony to back this up because he's about to tell an interesting story."],[2264.33,"But these are the folks."],[2265.77,"Now, uh just a brief comment on poor Secundus."],[2268.97,"Secundus. I say Por because his name means second or two"],[2277.41,"This was actually very common in the Roman world."],[2280.45,"Um we mentioned before that uh Roman names, you had four names, and only one of them was your given actual personal name."],[2291.17,"You had your father's name, you had a family name, and you had uh your grandfather or a patron's name."],[2300.02,"And then you had your actual name."],[2302.9,"Okay. Um this is so true that a lot of the names we know of for people, like we think of him as Caesar or Julius Caesar, his name was actually Gaius."],[2315.14,"And so was Caesar"],[2316.62,"That was his actual given name."],[2318.46,"Julius was a family name."],[2322.86,"Caesar's a title, wasn't it?"],[2324.14,"Or was it just Caesar became a title later?"],[2326.86,"Oh, okay. Yeah."],[2327.9,"Well, it was sort of a title."],[2329.58,"It was, they were all claiming his family name."],[2334.62,"Even if they weren't related to him."],[2336.78,"They were claiming to be related to him."],[2340.14,"Um. But yeah, so a lot of the Cicero name was not."],[2345.98,"Cicero is his family name."],[2347.74,"Oh wow And so because sort of your family name and your father's name were considered more important, you could really tell the people who had an egomaniac father because it was very common for their children, their sons to be named Primus, Secundus, Tertius, Quadratus."],[2369.7,"Octavia. So they just gave them, they just numbered them."],[2377.27,"Well, you know, William III."],[2381.27,"I mean, it's but so that's the Chinese do have a sort of system of number one son, number two son."],[2388.07,"I I taught two brothers who had the same name."],[2392.31,"Yeah. And uh they're they were given the nickname by the other students of Wang, and so they were called Wang and T Wang."],[2403.35,"But their name was was actually Sio"],[2407.71,"And they were both Sio, and there was no way of distinguishing they had exactly the same thing."],[2414.35,"Yeah. So Secundas, that was actually a Primus and Secundas and Tertius, especially, since that was one through three."],[2420.83,"We're very common given names."],[2424.03,"And you'll see them if you ever look through the lists of saints for a given day."],[2429.07,"And you're looking at martyrs and stuff in the early church, you'll see a lot of Primus's and secunduses and tertiuses and quadratuses."],[2435.47,"Okay. Because those were their like you were saying, it it seemed like the only thing that meant that was long or everlasting was conquering some sort of legacy, some sort of that."],[2449.59,"So if you didn't distinguish yourself that way, you were primus."],[2453.28,"And if and if you did distinguish yourself that way, see, then your name would be taken by your descendants to identify them with you, like Caesar."],[2462.64,"Yeah. Um, so yeah, that that was how that worked."],[2468.16,"But until you made a name for yourself that way, uh it was what family you belonged to was more important than who you were."],[2477.04,"Okay. As an individual."],[2479.86,"And that's sort of expressed in the naming uh convention."],[2485.46,"And again, this is a place where Christianity sort of runs up against that culture and says, No, God knows who you are."],[2492.76,"And you are the one who are gonna is gonna stand before Christ's judgment seat on the last day to give an account for what you have done in your life, and it doesn't matter what family you're from, or what tribe or what in the Middle Ages people had personal names only, and what we call last names developed much later, yeah."],[2513.78,"Wow. So, yeah, that's a major shift again."],[2521.06,"Listen next time as Father Stephen DeYoung continues his study of the scriptures on the Whole Counsel of God."],[2528.42,"Father Stephen's email address is wholecounsel at ancientfaith.com."],[2533.78,"That's wholecounsel at ancientfaith.com."]],"o":[{"t":56.96,"lv":2,"h":"Recap: Paul at Ephesus, Artemis","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Paul on his <b>third missionary journey</b>; came to <b>Ephesus</b> last time</li><li class=\"d0\">Visual aid: tourist <b>chotchke</b> replica of <b>Artemis of Ephesus</b></li><li class=\"d1\">looks different from other Artemis statues - \"schmushed together\" with <b>Eastern mother goddess</b> figures</li><li class=\"d0\">Rome <b>assimilated</b> religions of conquered territory</li><li class=\"d1\">\"these are really all the same\" gods under different names</li><li class=\"d1\">strong approach for <b>religiously unifying</b> a diverse empire</li><li class=\"d1\">let local festivals go on, file it all as \"one big thing\"</li><li class=\"d0\">\"Paganism\" is no one religion - <b>hodgepodge</b> of polytheistic systems from all over</li><li class=\"d0\">we see it as united precisely because of the <b>Romans'</b> propaganda</li></ul>"},{"t":194.48,"lv":2,"h":"Why Ephesus rioted: religion, economy, empire","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\"><b>Temple of Artemis</b> = one of the <b>seven wonders</b> of the ancient world</li><li class=\"d1\">people came from all over Rome for <b>festivals</b>, celebrations, to buy replicas</li><li class=\"d1\">huge part of the city's <b>industry</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Paul and company there <b>a year and a half</b>, preaching in synagogue and <b>publicly</b></li><li class=\"d1\">converts stop going to the temple, <b>get rid of idols</b>, stop buying from the craftsmen</li><li class=\"d1\">hurts the local economy → <b>craftsmen's guild</b> rises up, runs them out</li><li class=\"d0\">Exhibit A: we have <b>separation of church and state</b> - politics, economics, religion as separate things</li><li class=\"d0\">Ancient world: <b>all interwoven</b></li><li class=\"d1\">loyalty to Caesar = partaking in religious ceremonies = major part of the <b>local economy</b> = functioning of the empire</li><li class=\"d0\">Stays intertwined for many centuries</li></ul>"},{"t":334.46,"lv":2,"h":"Meat offered to idols","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Comes back in <b>1 Corinthians</b> - an issue everywhere in the empire for <b>Gentile converts</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Almost all meat in the <b>meat markets</b> (Corinth, Athens) came from animals sacrificed at temples <b>that day</b></li><li class=\"d1\">continually sacrificing multiple animals every day</li><li class=\"d0\">Greeks called it the <b>\"burning of the bones\"</b></li><li class=\"d1\">only the parts they had no other use for went to the god</li><li class=\"d1\">leather sold, meat sold - \"we got nothing to do with the bones, burn those\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Your <b>food supply</b> is tied to the pagan temples</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Acts 15</b>: Gentile converts told not to eat meat <b>offered to idols</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Paul has to deal with it</li></ul>"},{"t":420.46,"lv":3,"h":"Banquet halls and Daniel's vegetarianism","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Excavated <b>temple of Demeter at Corinth</b>: huge <b>banquet halls</b> for feasts on sacrificial meat</li><li class=\"d0\">Obvious part: can't go to the temple feast once Christian</li><li class=\"d0\">Less obvious: the meat in the market came from the same place</li><li class=\"d0\">Any other source of meat in those cities? <b>not really</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Paul will speak of <b>those who eat only vegetables</b></li><li class=\"d1\">not without precedent: <b>Daniel</b> and his three friends became <b>vegetarians</b> in Babylon</li><li class=\"d1\">palace meat all offered to <b>pagan gods</b></li></ul>"},{"t":490.45,"lv":2,"h":"Q&amp;A: what was a pagan sacrifice like?","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Q: What was a sacrifice like?</li><li class=\"d0\">Mostly <b>pigs</b> - most common in Greek and Roman sacrifice</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>breed fast and grow fast</b></li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Bulls</b> for major occasions, wealthy people, visiting <b>senator, emperor, general</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Day-to-day = pigs</li><li class=\"d0\">First a <b>procession</b> leading the animal to the place of killing</li><li class=\"d1\">led by the <b>priest</b>; women and others playing instruments, singing <b>hymns</b></li></ul>"},{"t":565.86,"lv":3,"h":"Hair in the fire, killing, and the prothesis","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Ceremony with a <b>fire</b>: cut a clump of the animal's <b>hair</b>, throw it in the fire pot</li><li class=\"d1\">carried into our <b>baptismal service</b>: hair cut and thrown into the censer</li><li class=\"d1\">symbolism: parents offering the child / person <b>offering themselves</b> to God - \"we don't kill them afterwards\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Animal killed with a knife, <b>slit throat</b>; <b>blood</b> drained into bowls</li><li class=\"d0\">Taken to the <b>prothesis</b> = \"place of preparation\"</li><li class=\"d1\">not a coincidence: table behind the <b>iconostasis</b> where we prepare bread and wine is the prothesis</li><li class=\"d0\">Butchered; parts separated:</li><li class=\"d1\">to the <b>priests</b>; <b>useless parts</b> burnt for the god; back to those who brought it</li><li class=\"d1\">to the <b>banquet hall</b>; to the <b>meat market</b></li></ul>"},{"t":684.18,"lv":3,"h":"The haruspex and omens","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Romans (less so Greeks) had a <b>haruspex</b> to inspect the <b>entrails</b></li><li class=\"d1\">looked for <b>omens</b>, predicting the future</li><li class=\"d0\">Tied to what the sacrifice was for: going into <b>battle</b> → \"things look good / bad\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Daily sacrifices: would the <b>god accept it</b>?</li><li class=\"d1\">if not, figure out the problem: \"must be these <b>Christians</b>\" - before that \"these <b>Jews</b> who aren't participating\"</li><li class=\"d0\">We have <b>stone livers</b> with markings for <b>training</b> these guys</li><li class=\"d1\">livers, kidneys, hearts marked up: \"here's what to look for\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Offered parts taken to the <b>altar</b> and burned; rest distributed</li></ul>"},{"t":782.58,"lv":2,"h":"Hero shrines and feeding the dead","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Greek and Roman temples had <b>two sections</b></li><li class=\"d1\">one for the <b>god</b> in question</li><li class=\"d1\">one a <b>shrine to a local hero</b> - where we get the word \"hero\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Hero = usually <b>founder of the city</b>, or mythical figure (<b>Perseus</b>; <b>Pericles</b> was real)</li><li class=\"d1\">shrine usually where they were <b>buried</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Greeks and Romans: at death you go to <b>Hades</b>, exist as a <b>shade</b> = \"shadow\", \"a shadow of your former self\"</li><li class=\"d1\">exists <b>as long as people remember you</b>; forgotten → cease to exist</li><li class=\"d0\">Like ancient Israel: <b>blood contains life</b> - hot when it comes out, cools when dead</li><li class=\"d0\">So they poured sacrificial blood on the hero's <b>grave</b> to <b>reinvigorate</b> the shade</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Homer's Odyssey</b>: Odysseus needs to talk to <b>Achilles</b></li><li class=\"d1\">cave gateway to Hades, sacrifices <b>black goats</b>, drains blood into a pit</li><li class=\"d1\">shades come lapping it up → draws out Achilles</li><li class=\"d1\">\"they gave it to the dead\" - even creepier</li><li class=\"d0\">Remember for <b>1-2 Corinthians</b>: food offered to idols, things involving the dead</li></ul>"},{"t":1002.05,"lv":2,"h":"Q&amp;A: other offerings and the pork problem","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Q: Crops or fruits - did they do that? <b>Less than the Jews</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Romans did a lot with <b>meal cakes</b></li><li class=\"d1\">not always meat, not always killing an animal - but <b>always food</b></li><li class=\"d1\">unsure how much banquet-hall food was sacrificed vs. just served alongside</li><li class=\"d0\">Israel's sacrificial animals: <b>sheep and goats</b></li><li class=\"d1\">Israelites can't eat <b>pork</b> → no pig sacrifice</li><li class=\"d0\">Jews in Greek cities where most meat on sale is <b>pork</b> → a problem</li><li class=\"d0\">Gentiles <b>not told to refrain from pork</b> (Acts 15) - but <b>forbidden meat offered to idols</b></li><li class=\"d1\">Question: does that include meat in the market, where <b>I'm not at the temple</b>, not offering to an idol?</li></ul>"},{"t":1122.65,"lv":3,"h":"Meat supply, and nobody sacrificed fish","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Q: Was temple meat a large part of the diet? In Corinth, Athens it was <b>pretty much the only meat</b> (unless a farmer)</li><li class=\"d1\">not wealthy → means <b>vegetarian</b> or <b>fish exclusively</b>, if you could afford it</li><li class=\"d0\">Interesting: <b>nobody ever sacrificed fish</b> - even to <b>water gods</b> like Poseidon</li><li class=\"d1\">theory: fish aren't <b>warm-blooded</b>; they linked <b>life with warm blood</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Also behind our <b>fasting rules</b>: fish not considered meat</li><li class=\"d1\">\"seafood wasn't considered meat in ancient times\" - \"this other kind of thing\"</li><li class=\"d0\">\"Here in Louisiana especially\"</li></ul>"},{"t":1244.05,"lv":2,"h":"The liturgy as bloodless sacrifice","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Everything around the <b>prothesis</b> is related</li><li class=\"d0\">Eucharist celebrated <b>as a sacrifice</b></li><li class=\"d1\">liturgical \"<b>unbloody service</b>\" hides it - really <b>bloodless sacrifice</b></li><li class=\"d1\">not killing anything: <b>Christ died once for all</b>, not killed again</li><li class=\"d0\">We offer a <b>thank offering</b></li><li class=\"d1\">we think all OT sacrifices were <b>sin offerings</b> killing animals - not true</li><li class=\"d1\">also <b>grain, drink, first-fruits</b> offerings</li><li class=\"d1\">Christ offered himself as sin offering for the whole world, all time</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Eucharist</b> = thanksgiving: offer <b>baked bread and wine</b>, receive <b>body and blood</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Prothesis follows the OT pattern</li><li class=\"d1\">piece cut out for the Eucharist; part <b>eaten by the priest</b>, part by the people, part <b>offered to God</b></li><li class=\"d1\"><b>Great Entrance</b>: process in with it to the altar</li><li class=\"d0\">An ancient Greek or Roman would say: \"they're <b>sacrificing bread and wine</b> to their God\"</li></ul>"},{"t":1418.76,"lv":3,"h":"From the Jewish temple, not pagan rites","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">We got it from the <b>sacrificial rites of the temple</b>, not pagan ones</li><li class=\"d0\">Temple procedure <b>not so radically different</b> from other sacrifices - Greeks, Romans, Babylonians recognized it</li><li class=\"d1\">not weird to them: <b>how</b> they sacrificed</li><li class=\"d1\">weird to them: <b>one temple, one God</b></li><li class=\"d1\">Roman general <b>Pompey</b> in the <b>Holy of Holies</b>: no image of a god - \"that was weird\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Recent scholars, even <b>non-Orthodox</b>: traditional Eastern liturgy and early Latin liturgy <b>reflect the Jewish temple</b>, even in details</li><li class=\"d1\">difference: done <b>everywhere</b>, not one place; <b>no animals</b> killed</li></ul>"},{"t":1524.43,"lv":2,"h":"Pagan worship was not exclusive","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Some Roman authors thought Jews and Christians were <b>atheists</b></li><li class=\"d1\">they refuse to <b>acknowledge the other gods</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Greeks and Romans might have one god they primarily served - but acknowledged <b>all</b></li><li class=\"d1\">visiting <b>Ephesus</b>: pay respects, offer a <b>pig at Artemis' temple</b></li><li class=\"d1\">not \"if I do this for Artemis I'm betraying <b>Mars</b>\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Pantheons with interrelations = how we learned it as kids; <b>not the reality</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Most had a god or two based on <b>where they were from</b> - cities had a god or two</li><li class=\"d1\">and <b>family</b>: \"grandpa <b>Gaius</b> went to war, offered a sacrifice, made it back\" → family honors that god</li><li class=\"d0\">Local heroes played a role too - honored, <b>images</b>, not worshipped directly</li></ul>"},{"t":1641.72,"lv":2,"h":"The cost of converting","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Become Christian: \"I'm not sacrificing to that god\" → \"what about <b>Grandpa Gaius</b>? You're <b>spitting on our family</b>\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Early Christians <b>thrown out of families and communities</b></li><li class=\"d1\">everyone in the city goes to the <b>festival</b></li><li class=\"d1\">couldn't even <b>sell at the market</b></li><li class=\"d1\">\"complete <b>social pariahs</b>\"</li><li class=\"d0\">We take it for granted: \"pick a church and go\"</li><li class=\"d1\">some families still staunchly something - leave and \"you're gone\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Stakes higher: affects <b>future well-being of the family</b>, \"endangering our city, <b>the empire</b>\"</li><li class=\"d1\">\"get away from us\" - or \"we need to <b>get rid of you</b>\" to make the gods happy</li><li class=\"d0\">Keep in mind through <b>Acts</b>: how high the stakes are for pagan converts</li><li class=\"d1\">and the <b>Jewish community</b> too: crisis at the <b>start of the second century</b> when synagogues <b>expel all Christians</b> - already starting</li><li class=\"d0\">Christ: \"he who loves <b>father or mother</b> more than me is not worthy of me\" - a reality for Jewish and pagan converts</li></ul>"},{"t":1841.0,"lv":2,"h":"Revolutionary: one God who knows your name","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Here's <b>one God</b> who controls the universe, cares, <b>sacrifices himself</b> for us - truly shocking</li><li class=\"d0\">\"One God who created the universe and <b>knows your name</b>\" and loves you</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>regardless of status</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Greek and Roman gods <b>didn't know who you were</b></li><li class=\"d1\">might know the emperor - and could go either way, could <b>destroy you</b></li><li class=\"d1\">most human interactions \"did not go well for the human\"</li><li class=\"d1\">chained to a rock, pushing a rock up a hill</li></ul>"},{"t":1907.52,"lv":2,"h":"Martyrs impress the Roman world","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Romans see Christians of all backgrounds <b>willing to suffer and die</b> rather than turn back</li><li class=\"d1\">none of them would die if told \"now worship <b>Athena</b> instead of Artemis\"</li><li class=\"d1\">\"some truth you're willing to be <b>tortured to death</b> rather than deny\" → <b>curiosity</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Q: executioner converting? Yes, or <b>bystanders</b></li></ul>"},{"t":1968.34,"lv":3,"h":"Coptic martyrs and the seed of the church","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Goes on today: <b>Coptic Christians martyred by ISIS</b> on the beach</li><li class=\"d1\">icon in his office; one is <b>darker-complexioned</b> - he was an <b>ISIS member</b></li><li class=\"d1\">saw them die, heard them refuse to deny; said \"<b>their God is my God</b>\"</li><li class=\"d1\">camera turned on him: \"what did you say?\" - repeated it, knelt next to them, <b>killed too</b></li><li class=\"d1\">\"willing to kill for what I believe, but not sure I'd be willing to <b>get killed</b>\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Early church saying: <b>blood of the martyrs became the seed of the church</b></li><li class=\"d1\">more they stamped out Christianity, the more it grew</li><li class=\"d1\">already in Acts: <b>St Stephen</b> stoned → everyone scattered → <b>churches spring up everywhere</b></li></ul>"},{"t":2085.02,"lv":2,"h":"Acts 20:1-3 - Macedonia and Greece","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\"><b>Acts 20:1</b>: after the <b>uproar</b> in Ephesus, Paul calls the disciples, embraces them, departs for <b>Macedonia</b></li><li class=\"d1\">Ephesus on the western coast of <b>Asia Minor</b>; sails across to <b>northern Greece</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Retracing his route from the last journey</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>20:2-3</b>: encouraged them with many words, came to <b>Greece</b>, stayed <b>three months</b></li><li class=\"d1\">revisiting churches he planted, <b>encouraging and preaching</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Jews plot against him as he's about to sail to <b>Syria</b> → returns through <b>Macedonia</b></li><li class=\"d1\">original plan: back to <b>Antioch</b>, his home base</li><li class=\"d1\">some in the synagogue still unhappy, planning something → go back the way he came</li><li class=\"d0\">Again: Paul is <b>not trying to cause</b> these issues</li></ul>"},{"t":2198.01,"lv":2,"h":"Acts 20:4 - Travel companions","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Companions: <b>Sopater of Berea</b>, <b>Aristarchus</b> and <b>Secundus</b> of the Thessalonians, <b>Gaius of Derbe</b>, <b>Timothy</b>, <b>Tychicus</b> and <b>Trophimus</b> of Asia</li><li class=\"d1\">many from cities he has visited</li><li class=\"d0\">Q: Romanesque church of <b>Saint Trophime</b> in France - same name</li><li class=\"d0\">Luke isn't just being thorough</li><li class=\"d1\">writing as a <b>historian</b>: these people were <b>still alive</b> when he wrote</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>witnesses</b> to the story he's about to tell - \"you could go ask these people\"</li></ul>"},{"t":2265.77,"lv":3,"h":"Secundus and Roman naming","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Poor <b>Secundus</b> - name means <b>second</b></li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Roman names</b>: four names, only one was your <b>personal name</b></li><li class=\"d1\">father's name, family name, grandfather or patron's name, then your own</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>Julius Caesar</b> = given name <b>Gaius</b>; <b>Julius</b> family name; <b>Caesar</b> sort of a title - all claiming his family name</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>Cicero</b> = family name</li><li class=\"d0\">Family and father's name mattered more → <b>egomaniac fathers</b> named sons <b>Primus, Secundus, Tertius, Quadratus</b></li><li class=\"d1\">like \"William III\"; Chinese number-one son, number-two son</li><li class=\"d1\">very common given names; see lots of them in <b>lists of martyrs</b> in the saints' lives</li><li class=\"d0\">Until you made a name, <b>what family you belonged to</b> mattered more than who you were</li></ul>"},{"t":2485.46,"lv":3,"h":"Christianity: God knows you as an individual","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Christianity runs against that culture: \"<b>God knows who you are</b>\"</li><li class=\"d0\">You stand before <b>Christ's judgment seat</b> to account for your own life</li><li class=\"d1\">doesn't matter what <b>family, tribe</b> you're from</li><li class=\"d0\">Middle Ages: people had <b>personal names only</b>; last names much later</li><li class=\"d0\">\"A major shift again\"</li></ul>"}]});
